From 2ac08f13ef33279152dd2f07f6424edebd5517a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: seedds <seedds@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 09:09:02 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] 0137: in-game HUD editor runtime -- write the pane GLOBAL
 matrix (+0x70) directly at the +0x13c cut, true hide via flags bit 0

0135 proved on device (log-46) that shifting the pane LOCAL never reaches
the display: log deltas 6/6 correct, screenshot pixel-identical to V1.3.17.
The .bflyt analysis explains why (display = layout-instance placement +
pane-chain global; a static pane's +0x70 is never composed, so the local
shift has nothing to compose into). 0137 writes +0x70 itself at the +0x13c
cut, where the pane's own global matrix is final and before both child
loops, re-derived from a stored ORIGINAL global (e_mod) plus the effective
delta, with the local still written as fallback. Hide is a TRUE hide
(flags bit 0 cleared), not +10000 off-screen, per the .bflyt finding that
flags=0x00 panes are never drawn. It applies ONLY the editor manual deltas
from the state page on top of the 0136 overlay base -- no auto-edge term,
so the edge is never applied twice. No probe on this path.
---
 dm/games/0100000000010000/hud_runtime.c | 679 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 dm/games/0100000000010000/patches.py    |  93 +++-
 2 files changed, 770 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 dm/games/0100000000010000/hud_runtime.c

diff --git a/dm/games/0100000000010000/hud_runtime.c b/dm/games/0100000000010000/hud_runtime.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3898601
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dm/games/0100000000010000/hud_runtime.c
@@ -0,0 +1,679 @@
+// hud_edge.c -- Super Mario Odyssey HUD edge anchor + launcher manual settings (0118 + 0119 + 0120 + 0123 + 0126 + 0127 + 0130 + 0132 + 0135).
+//
+// The game's HUD (coins, moons, compass, lives, hints) is laid out for a 1280x720
+// (16:9) screen, so on a wider screen (iPhone 19.5:9) it sits inset from the
+// edges, and on a narrower screen (iPhone Duo 1.43:1, iPad 4:3) it would be
+// cut off at the edges. By default this hook pushes the HUD to the screen
+// edges, automatically adapting to the screen aspect (0118): the left widgets
+// (coins, regional coins, moons) move left on a wider screen and right on a narrower one; the
+// right widgets (compass, lives, hints) move right on a wider screen and left
+// on a narrower one. A manual setting made in the launcher's HUD editor (0119)
+// is a delta from that automatic edge anchor (0128): the editor shows the
+// element at its edge-anchored default and the player drags from there, so the
+// manual offset is added to the edge -- dragging in the editor moves the
+// element by the same amount in the game -- while elements with no manual
+// setting keep following the aspect. A hidden element is moved off-screen, so
+// it is not drawn. The compass takes one extra offset (0128): its pane
+// 'PicStrap' is a 340-wide strip and the visible dial is centred in it, so
+// the plain edge puts the strip at the screen edge but the dial ~128 short
+// of it; the extra offset brings the dial (and the hints text in the strip)
+// to the edge, matching the editor, which centres the compass on the lives
+// column.
+//
+// The automatic offset comes from the screen aspect the host already writes
+// into the frame-step state page (loader.c dm_fps_state_apply, state +0x58:
+// the ratio r; the iOS front end resolves DM_SCREEN_ASPECT "auto" to the
+// window's aspect before the host writes the state page, so r is the resolved
+// aspect by the time the game runs; 0 means 16:9). The offset is:
+//     edge = 640 * (r / 1.7777778 - 1)
+// iPhone 19.5:9 (r=2.167): +140 per side (push out). iPhone Duo 1.43:1
+// (r=1.429): -126 per side (pull in). iPad 4:3 (r=1.333): -160 per side
+// (pull in). 16:9 (r=0): 0. The sign is uniform: a left widget takes
+// dx = -edge, a right widget dx = +edge, so a wider screen pushes both
+// outward and a narrower screen pulls both inward.
+//
+// The manual settings come from the launcher, through the host, in the
+// frame-step state page at +0x200 (hud_manual.c, written by
+// dm_hud_manual_frame_tick once per present; the launcher sets the
+// DM_HUD_COMPASS / DM_HUD_LIFE / DM_HUD_COINS / DM_HUD_MOONS / DM_HUD_HINTS
+// environment variables from the player's editor choices before the game starts):
+//   +0x200: 15 floats, per element (dx, dy, k): compass, lives, coins,
+//     moons, hints. dx / dy are layout points (the 1280x720 space), a DELTA
+//     from the automatic edge anchor (0128): the launcher's editor shows the
+//     element at its edge-anchored default and records the drag from there,
+//     so the manual offset is ADDED to the edge -- dragging in the editor
+//     moves the element by the same amount in the game. A drag down the
+//     screen is a negative dy (the layout is y-up); the launcher negates its
+//     y-down drag when it writes the value. k is the element's size, 0.6 to
+//     1.8 (the editor's slider), 1 is the game's own size.
+//   +0x240: u32 manual mask (bit i: element i has a manual setting).
+//   +0x244: u32 hidden mask (bit i: element i is hidden).
+// The state page starts zeroed, so an unwritten block reads as no manual
+// setting and nothing hidden, and a zero / non-number k reads as 1.
+// A manual setting is per element and independent: moving the coins does not
+// move the moons, and hiding one element leaves the others alone.
+// (0123 moved the masks from +0x230/+0x234: 15 floats occupy +0x200..+0x23B,
+// so the old mask slots would have collided with the fifth element's data.)
+//
+// The hook runs from the same two rewriter cuts as the old 0113
+// (Pane::Calculate main+0x909494: the entry cut at +0x20 and the +0x13c cut
+// at +0x13c), because one cut misses static panes (see the old hud_calc.c
+// header: the skip path over the +0x13c site). It matches the five HUD
+// widgets by pane name only -- no subtree walk, no tree search, no
+// guide/root classification, no camera widening, no log. The only walk is the
+// dirty-mark for a moved container's children (hud_edge_mark_dirty), which
+// only sets a flag and uses the v8 single struct-array (no second base
+// register, so the log-27 sdepth/x18 fault class is structurally absent);
+// it never reads a pane name and never decides a match.
+//
+// (0137) Runtime HUD control for the in-game editor: the DISPLAYED
+// position is moved by writing the pane's GLOBAL matrix (+0x70) directly,
+// not by shifting the local and hoping the game recomposes it. 0135 proved
+// on device (log-46) that the local shift never reaches the display: the
+// log deltas were 6/6 correct yet the screenshot was pixel-identical to
+// V1.3.17. The .bflyt analysis (REPORT-HUD-BFLYT.md) explains why: the
+// display position is the layout-instance placement plus the pane-chain
+// global, and a static pane's +0x70 is never composed by the game, so the
+// local shift has nothing to compose into. 0137 writes +0x70 itself, at the
+// +0x13c cut where the comment in patches.py says the pane's own global
+// matrix is final and before both child loops -- so the written global is
+// what the children compose from and what Draw renders. The write is
+// always re-derived from a stored ORIGINAL global (e_mod, +56..+104: the
+// 12 floats of +0x70 as first seen), plus the effective delta, so a fixed
+// offset cannot drift and a game recompute is followed by re-storing the
+// original. The local (+0x30/+0x48) is STILL written exactly as 0135 did:
+// it is the fallback for any path that recomposes from the local, and it
+// costs nothing. Hide is a TRUE hide here, not +10000: the pane's visible
+// bit (flags bit 0 at +0x58) is cleared to hide and set to show, per the
+// .bflyt finding that flags=0x00 panes are never drawn. (0136's overlay
+// still supplies the static auto-edge base positions in the .szs; 0137
+// applies the editor's live adjustments on top of them.)
+// A moved pane's original LOCAL is stored in a small table (state +0x300,
+// 96 128-byte entries, sixteen per element -- up to sixteen layout instances can
+// match the same element) and the shifted local is always re-derived from that
+// original, so a fixed offset never accumulates into a drift, and a game
+// recompute (an animation, a count change, a layout reload) is followed by
+// re-storing the original. The offset, size and hidden flag the local was
+// shifted by are also stored per entry, so a settings change or an aspect
+// change re-applies instead of being ignored, and a manual element is not
+// re-derived from the automatic offset.
+//
+// (0128) A manual setting is a delta from the automatic edge anchor, NOT an
+// absolute offset from the game's default layout. The old (0119-0127) code
+// treated it as absolute, replacing the edge -- but the editor shows the
+// element at its edge-anchored default and records the drag from there, so
+// a manual element landed one whole edge (140 on iPhone) away from where
+// the player dragged it (V1.3.17, log-33: coins manual 16,-3 landed at
+// default+16 instead of edge+16; the moons manual 293,169 likewise). The
+// manual offset is now ADDED to the edge anchor, so dragging in the editor
+// moves the element by the same amount in the game, and a manual element
+// still follows the aspect (as the editor does). An old saved setting (recorded
+// as a drag from the edge) is now read correctly with no migration.
+//
+// Parent compensation (0123, revised 0135): a child of a matched parent
+// must not be double-shifted. The old design undid the parent's transform
+// from the child's global matrix (hud_edge_unparent); the local design is
+// simpler: a pane's displayed shift is its parent's applied shift plus its
+// own local shift, so the child's local delta is its own target minus the
+// parent's applied delta (read from the parent's table entry -- a parent
+// with no entry, or a fresh entry, applied the identity, and the parent's
+// Calculate runs before the child's, so its entry is already written when
+// the child is processed). The scale is the ratio: the child's displayed
+// scale is the parent's applied scale times the child's local scale, so the
+// local scale is the target scale divided by the parent's k. A child that
+// wants the same transform as its parent takes a zero local delta and rides
+// the parent -- the old (c) "same transform" gate is gone, subsumed by the
+// compensation (a zero effective delta is just the already-correct early
+// return). A hidden parent's +10000 is in its applied delta, so a child of
+// a hidden parent is pulled back on-screen by exactly that amount (hiding
+// the parent does not hide the child); a hidden child of a visible parent
+// is pushed off-screen.
+//
+// What this patch does NOT do (deliberately): no map-camera widen, no
+// settings/map code, no guide-text hiding, no root stretch. The camera icon
+// in the top-right is NOT a HUD element -- it is the photo button (a
+// virtual pad button, positioned by DmGamepad), so it is never matched.
+// The dots under the moon counter move with the moons element: they are part
+// of the counter's layout (the 'Counter' group), so the launcher's moons
+// element draws them with the moon icon and number, and moving the moons
+// element carries them. The white bar under the counts is drawn with the
+// coins element in the launcher; its own pane is not matched by name -- no
+// pane in any log or in the DmIOS string pool matches a wide thin bar under
+// the counts (the coins group's Capture00-03 are 128x128 squares, WinLine is
+// a 452x38 line under the guide text), so whether it follows the coins in
+// the game depends on its parent, which the available logs do not show --
+// it may stay where the game put it (a known limitation, 0123).
+// The regional-coins icon and number (PicIconSh / PicIcon / TxtTotalShine under a
+// 'Counter') are their OWN element (0127: the player splits the two kinds of
+// coins into two separately adjustable groups -- coins = the coin icon + number,
+// regional coins = the hexagon icon + number, moons = the dots). The moons element
+// is the 'Counter' group itself (which carries the dots). A bare
+// 'TxtTotalShine' whose parent is NOT a 'Counter' stays the moons fallback.
+// 'PicIcon' also appears elsewhere (the lives cluster, the cursor group),
+// so it is only matched when its parent is a 'Counter' -- any other parent
+// is left alone.
+//
+// Compiled with: aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc -O2 -ffreestanding -fno-builtin
+//   -fno-stack-protector -fno-jump-tables -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -c
+// (same flags as the old hud_calc.c). The function takes everything through
+// its arguments -- no static data, no literal pool, no external calls -- so
+// the emitted words are position-independent and relocation-free and can be
+// dropped into the rewriter's patch area verbatim:
+//   x0 = the pane (nn::ui2d::Pane *), x1 = the aspect (state + 0x58: a float,
+//   the screen's width / height; 0 = 16:9), x2 = the edge table (state +
+//   0x300: 96 128-byte entries, sixteen per HUD element), x3 = the manual
+//   settings (state + 0x200: 18 floats, a u32 manual mask, a u32 hidden
+//   mask; a NULL pointer reads as no manual setting).
+//   Element order: 0 compass (right), 1 lives (right), 2 coins (left),
+//   3 moons (left), 4 hints (right), 5 regional coins (left). The state page starts zeroed, so a table entry with
+//   pane == 0 is unused.
+
+typedef unsigned int u32;
+typedef unsigned long uintptr_t;
+
+#define HUD_EDGE_N 6
+#define HUD_EDGE_ENTRY 128         // bytes per table entry
+#define HUD_EDGE_SLOTS_PER_ELEM 16  // table slots per HUD element: up to sixteen layout instances can match the same element (log-20 shows TEN panes matching coins -- 2x TxtCounterSh + 2x TxtCounter + 2x PicIconSh + 2x PicIcon + 2x TxtTotalShine, two Counter groups; eight slots thrashed and the re-init re-derived the original from an already-modified matrix, so the offset drifted off-screen -- 0126; 0120 raised it 2 -> 8 for the four number panes alone)
+
+// The manual settings block (state +0x200), as the host writes it.
+#define HUD_MANUAL_FLOATS 18       // dx, dy, k per element (6 elements)
+#define HUD_MANUAL_MASK_OFF 72     // byte offset of the manual mask (+0x248; the 18 floats end at +0x247)
+#define HUD_HIDDEN_MASK_OFF 76     // byte offset of the hidden mask (+0x24C)
+
+// A hidden element is pushed this far off-screen (0113 hid its guide text
+// the same way: a pane already past 5000 is off-screen). The layout is at
+// most 1560 wide, so +10000 is safely outside it whatever the aspect.
+#define HUD_HIDE_DX 10000.0f
+
+// The compass dial is centred in its 340-wide 'PicStrap' strip, so the plain
+// edge puts the strip at the screen edge but the dial ~128 short of it (0128:
+// log-20 gives the strip as 340x720; the V1.3.17 screenshot puts the dial's
+// centre ~175 from the strip's left edge, i.e. centred, and the editor
+// centres the compass on the lives column). This extra offset, added to the
+// compass's edge anchor on every aspect, brings the dial (and the hints text
+// in the strip) to the edge. It is a property of the strip's layout (the dial
+// centred in 340), not of the aspect, so it is a constant. The strip's right
+// edge then sits ~100 past the screen edge -- the strip is transparent there,
+// only the dial and the hints text are visible.
+#define HUD_COMPASS_EXTRA 128.0f
+
+// The pane names, as little-endian u32s (no string literal: a literal would
+// sit in .rodata and the code would load its address with an adrp, a
+// relocation, and the embedded words carry no literal pool).
+// "PicStrap": "PicS", "trap" (8 letters, the 9th byte is the NUL).
+#define HUD_E_STRAP0 0x53636950u
+#define HUD_E_STRAP1 0x70617274u
+// "Counter": "Coun", "ter\0".
+#define HUD_E_COUNTER0 0x6e756f43u
+#define HUD_E_COUNTER1 0x00726574u
+// "TxtTotalShine": "TxtT", "otal", "Shin", "e\0".
+#define HUD_E_TSHINE0 0x54747854u
+#define HUD_E_TSHINE1 0x6c61746fu
+#define HUD_E_TSHINE2 0x6e696853u
+#define HUD_E_TSHINE3 0x00000065u
+// "TxtCounterSh": "TxtC", "ount", "erSh", 0 (12 bytes, the fourth word is the NUL word).
+#define HUD_E_TCSH0 0x43747854u
+#define HUD_E_TCSH1 0x746e756fu
+#define HUD_E_TCSH2 0x68537265u
+#define HUD_E_TCSH3 0x00000000u
+// "TxtCounter": "TxtC", "ount", "er\0".
+#define HUD_E_TC0 0x43747854u
+#define HUD_E_TC1 0x746e756fu
+#define HUD_E_TC2 0x00007265u
+// "TxtLife": "TxtL", "ife\0".
+#define HUD_E_TLIFE0 0x4c747854u
+#define HUD_E_TLIFE1 0x00656669u
+// "Life" ("Life" as one word; the 5th byte is the NUL).
+#define HUD_E_LIFE0 0x6566694cu
+// "PicIconSh": "PicI", "conS", "h\0\0\0" (the moon icon's shadow picture,
+// under the moons 'Counter' group -- coins since 0123).
+#define HUD_E_PICONSH0 0x49636950u
+#define HUD_E_PICONSH1 0x536e6f63u
+#define HUD_E_PICONSH2 0x00000068u
+// "PicIcon": "PicI", "con\0" (the moon icon itself, under 'Counter').
+#define HUD_E_PICON0 0x49636950u
+#define HUD_E_PICON1 0x006e6f63u
+// "TxtGuide01": "TxtG", "uide", "01\0\0" (the "+ menu / - map" hint text,
+// under the compass 'PicStrap' strip in log-20 -- hints since 0123).
+#define HUD_E_TGUIDE0 0x47747854u
+#define HUD_E_TGUIDE1 0x65646975u
+#define HUD_E_TGUIDE2 0x00003130u
+
+// Bounded compare of a pane name's words against the given words (n words,
+// the rest of the name unchecked). Same as the old hud_words_eq.
+static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) int hud_edge_words_eq(const u32 *nw, u32 a, u32 b, u32 c, u32 d, int n) {
+    if (n >= 1 && nw[0] != a) return 0;
+    if (n >= 2 && nw[1] != b) return 0;
+    if (n >= 3 && nw[2] != c) return 0;
+    if (n >= 4 && nw[3] != d) return 0;
+    return 1;
+}
+
+// A manual size outside [0.6, 1.8], or not a number, is the editor's bug, not
+// a request: fall back to 1 (no resize). A zero scale is not a request either
+// (the state page starts zeroed, so an unwritten k reads as 0 and must mean
+// identity, never a collapse). The NaN test is k != k (a NaN is not equal to
+// itself); a negative scale would mirror the widget, which nobody asked for.
+// (The launcher's slider only writes 0.6 to 1.8; the host clamps too; this is
+// the third clamp, so a wild environment variable can never collapse a widget.)
+static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) float hud_edge_clamp_k(float k) {
+    if (k == 0.0f || k != k) return 1.0f;
+    if (k < 0.6f) return 0.6f;
+    if (k > 1.8f) return 1.8f;
+    return k;
+}
+
+// A manual offset outside the layout is the editor's bug, not a request:
+// clamp it, so a wild environment variable can never throw a widget off the
+// screen (hiding is the only way off-screen, and it is explicit).
+static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) float hud_edge_clamp_off(float v, float lim) {
+    if (v != v) return 0.0f;
+    if (v > lim) return lim;
+    if (v < -lim) return -lim;
+    return v;
+}
+
+// Find the table entry for a pane (a linear scan over the whole table: 80
+// entries, no pointer chasing). Returns 0 when the pane has no entry (it
+// was never matched, or its slot was reclaimed).
+static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) char *hud_edge_find_entry(void *table, void *pane) {
+    for (int i = 0; i < HUD_EDGE_N * HUD_EDGE_SLOTS_PER_ELEM; i++) {
+        char *e = (char *)table + i * HUD_EDGE_ENTRY;
+        if (*(u32 *)(e + 124) != 0 && *(uintptr_t *)(e + 0) == (uintptr_t)pane) return e;
+    }
+    return 0;
+}
+
+// The guards every subtree walk below applies (same as the old v7 guards,
+// because a layout's child list can go stale while the game unloads it):
+//  (a) a popped pane is checked for NULL / alignment before it is read;
+//  (b) a list node is checked (non-NULL, 8-byte aligned, >= 0x10000) before
+//      child = node - 8 is computed from it;
+//  (c) a child that is the pane itself, or lies inside the pane's own
+//      first 0x100 bytes, is never treated as a pane.
+static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) int hud_edge_node_ok(void *node) {
+    uintptr_t v = (uintptr_t)node;
+    return v != 0 && (v & 7u) == 0 && v >= 0x10000;
+}
+
+static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) int hud_edge_child_self(void *p, void *child) {
+    if (child == p) return 1;
+    if ((uintptr_t)child <= (uintptr_t)p) return 0;
+    return (uintptr_t)child - (uintptr_t)p < 0x100;
+}
+
+// The DFS stack: ONE array of {pane, depth} entries (the v8 fix for the
+// log-27 crash: the old v7 kept two parallel arrays, and the second array's
+// separate base register was the faulting access). Co-locating pane and depth
+// in one entry leaves a single array base, so there is no second base
+// register to lose.
+typedef struct { void *pane; unsigned char depth; } hud_edge_stack_ent;
+
+// Mark every descendant of a pane dirty (flags bit 4 at +0x58), so the game
+// recomputes their global matrices from this pane's (just-shifted) local.
+// This is the ONLY subtree walk in this patch, and it only sets a flag: it
+// never reads a pane name and never decides a match. The parent-changed flag
+// the game passes to children is 0 on the skip path, so without this a static
+// container's children never recompute and never follow it. The flag is the
+// game's own signal (Pane::Calculate sets it when a parent changed and
+// clears it on recompute), so the game's next Calculate on each descendant
+// recomputes from the moved parent. Bounded (64 visits, 32 children per
+// pane, depth 3); the pane itself is never marked here -- hud_edge marks it separately
+// after writing its local, so the game recomputes this pane's +0x70 from the
+// shifted local in the same Calculate call. Only called when the hook
+// actually writes a new local, never on the already-correct early return.
+static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) void hud_edge_mark_dirty(void *pane) {
+    if (!pane) return;
+    hud_edge_stack_ent st[64];
+    int sp = 0, visits = 0;
+    st[sp].pane = pane;
+    st[sp].depth = 3;
+    sp++;
+    while (sp > 0 && visits < 64) {
+        sp--;
+        void *p = st[sp].pane;
+        if (!p || ((uintptr_t)p & 7u)) continue;   // (a) never read a popped NULL / unaligned pane
+        unsigned char depth = st[sp].depth;
+        void *head = (char *)p + 0x20;
+        void *node = *(void **)((const char *)p + 0x28);
+        for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) {
+            if (!node || node == head) break;
+            if (!hud_edge_node_ok(node)) break;     // (b) never follow a bad node
+            void *child = (char *)node - 8;
+            if (hud_edge_child_self(p, child)) { node = *(void **)((const char *)child + 0x10); continue; } // (c) skip the self child, keep its siblings
+            visits++;
+            *(unsigned short *)((char *)child + 0x58) |= 0x10u;   // flags bit 4: dirty, recompute from the parent
+            if (depth > 0 && sp < 64) {
+                st[sp].pane = child;
+                st[sp].depth = depth - 1;
+                sp++;
+            }
+            node = *(void **)((const char *)child + 0x10);
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+// Is the given pane's parent named by these words (exact, n words)? The
+// parent is read once, directly -- no walk. A NULL parent is never the
+// container, so a parentless pane takes the fallback.
+static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) int hud_edge_parent_is(void *pane, u32 a, u32 b, int n) {
+    void *parent = *(void **)((const char *)pane + 0x18);
+    if (!parent) return 0;
+    const u32 *pnw = (const u32 *)((const char *)parent + 0xb8);
+    if (n >= 1 && pnw[0] != a) return 0;
+    if (n >= 2 && pnw[1] != b) return 0;
+    return 1;
+}
+
+void hud_edge(void *pane, const float *aspect_ptr, void *table, const float *params) {
+    if (!pane || !aspect_ptr || !table) return;
+    // The edge offset, from the screen aspect the host wrote into the state
+    // page (state +0x58; 0 = 16:9, unwritten). Valid aspects are 1.3 to 2.2
+    // (the range loader.c accepts); anything else is 16:9. The offset is
+    // clamped so a wild aspect can never throw a widget off the screen.
+    // A manual setting or a hide still applies when the edge is 0 (16:9),
+    // so the early return below is only the no-manual no-hide no-edge case.
+    float aspect = *aspect_ptr;
+    float edge = 0.0f;
+    if (aspect >= 1.3f && aspect <= 2.2f) {
+        edge = 640.0f * (aspect / 1.7777778f - 1.0f);
+        if (edge > 200.0f) edge = 200.0f;
+        if (edge < -200.0f) edge = -200.0f;
+    }
+
+    // The manual settings (state +0x200; a NULL params pointer, or an
+    // unwritten block, reads as no manual setting and nothing hidden).
+    u32 manual_mask = 0, hidden_mask = 0;
+    if (params) {
+        const u32 *pu = (const u32 *)params;
+        manual_mask = pu[HUD_MANUAL_MASK_OFF / 4];
+        hidden_mask = pu[HUD_HIDDEN_MASK_OFF / 4];
+    }
+    // 16:9 with no manual setting and nothing hidden: nothing to do for any
+    // element (the 0118 early return, kept so the common case costs nothing).
+    if (edge == 0.0f && manual_mask == 0 && hidden_mask == 0) return;
+
+    // Flags bit 7 is set by Pane::Calculate's epilogue, so it tells whether
+    // this pane has ever calculated. On a pane's very first frame its matrix
+    // is still zero; skip it (the +0x13c cut catches it after the game
+    // computes it, and the entry cut catches it from the next frame on).
+    unsigned short pflags = *(const unsigned short *)((const char *)pane + 0x58);
+    if (!(pflags & 0x80u)) return;
+
+    const char *name = (const char *)pane + 0xb8;
+    const u32 *nw = (const u32 *)name;
+
+    // Which HUD element is this pane, by name only (no subtree walk).
+    // Moons: the counter group itself, a pane named 'Counter'; a bare
+    // 'TxtTotalShine' is the fallback for a layout with no 'Counter' group,
+    // and only when its parent is not a 'Counter' (the group's own number
+    // follows the group; matching both would move it twice). Life: the
+    // 'Life' container, or a bare 'TxtLife' whose parent is NOT named
+    // 'Life'. Coins: 'TxtCounterSh' and its sibling 'TxtCounter', both
+    // matched by name, so both take the same offset and no twin-write walk
+    // is needed. Regional coins (0127): the hexagon icon and its number,
+    // which live UNDER the moons 'Counter' group: a 'PicIconSh' / 'PicIcon' /
+    // 'TxtTotalShine' whose parent IS a 'Counter' belongs to the regional
+    // coins element (its own element since 0127, so it can be dragged and
+    // hidden separately from the coins), and the parent-compensation step
+    // below keeps it from being moved twice. 'PicIcon' is also used
+    // elsewhere (the lives cluster, the cursor group), so it is only matched
+    // when its parent is a 'Counter'.
+    // Compass: 'PicStrap', the right strip (the dial itself is not a Pane,
+    // so the strip is the compass; moving the strip moves the whole
+    // right-hand cluster together). Hints: 'TxtGuide01', the "+ menu / - map"
+    // text that sits under the same 'PicStrap' strip in log-20 (its own
+    // element since 0123, so it can be dragged and hidden on its own; the
+    // parent compensation keeps it from following the compass). The old v1
+    // actor names (CompassArea / CoinCounter / MoonNum) are NOT matched:
+    // they are actor / state-machine names, not pane names, and matching
+    // them never fired.
+    int idx = -1;
+    if (nw[0] == HUD_E_STRAP0 && nw[1] == HUD_E_STRAP1 && name[8] == 0) idx = 0;
+    else if (nw[0] == HUD_E_LIFE0 && name[4] == 0) idx = 1;
+    else if (hud_edge_words_eq(nw, HUD_E_TLIFE0, HUD_E_TLIFE1, 0, 0, 2)) {
+        if (!hud_edge_parent_is(pane, HUD_E_LIFE0, 0, 1)) idx = 1;
+    }
+    else if (hud_edge_words_eq(nw, HUD_E_TCSH0, HUD_E_TCSH1, HUD_E_TCSH2, HUD_E_TCSH3, 4)) idx = 2;
+    else if (hud_edge_words_eq(nw, HUD_E_TC0, HUD_E_TC1, HUD_E_TC2, 0, 3)) idx = 2;
+    else if (hud_edge_words_eq(nw, HUD_E_PICONSH0, HUD_E_PICONSH1, HUD_E_PICONSH2, 0, 3)) {
+        if (hud_edge_parent_is(pane, HUD_E_COUNTER0, HUD_E_COUNTER1, 2)) idx = 5;
+    }
+    else if (hud_edge_words_eq(nw, HUD_E_PICON0, HUD_E_PICON1, 0, 0, 2)) {
+        if (hud_edge_parent_is(pane, HUD_E_COUNTER0, HUD_E_COUNTER1, 2)) idx = 5;
+    }
+    else if (hud_edge_words_eq(nw, HUD_E_COUNTER0, HUD_E_COUNTER1, 0, 0, 2)) idx = 3;
+    else if (hud_edge_words_eq(nw, HUD_E_TSHINE0, HUD_E_TSHINE1, HUD_E_TSHINE2, HUD_E_TSHINE3, 4)) {
+        if (hud_edge_parent_is(pane, HUD_E_COUNTER0, HUD_E_COUNTER1, 2)) idx = 5;
+        else idx = 3;
+    }
+    else if (hud_edge_words_eq(nw, HUD_E_TGUIDE0, HUD_E_TGUIDE1, HUD_E_TGUIDE2, 0, 3)) idx = 4;
+    if (idx < 0) return;   // not a HUD element: leave it alone
+
+    // What this element should be, this frame. Priority: hidden first, then
+    // manual, then the automatic edge anchor (0118). A manual setting is a
+    // DELTA from the automatic edge anchor (0128): the launcher's editor
+    // shows the element at its edge-anchored default and records the drag
+    // from there, so the manual offset is ADDED to the edge -- dragging in
+    // the editor moves the element by the same amount in the game -- and a
+    // manual element still follows the aspect (as the editor does). The
+    // compass's base anchor also carries the extra offset above (its dial is
+    // centred in the 340-wide strip). A hidden element is pushed off-screen (its
+    // local x +10000); its manual offset and size do not matter while it is
+    // hidden, and it comes back to its manual (or automatic) place when it is
+    // shown again. (0135) All three behaviours are DISPLAYED-position moves
+    // applied as local deltas -- edge, manual drag, and hide all go through
+    // the local translation/scale; +0x70 is never written.
+    // (0137) The runtime patch applies ONLY the editor's live adjustments
+    // on top of whatever base the layout already has. The auto-edge base
+    // positions come from the 0136 overlay (baked into the .szs .bflyt), NOT
+    // from this patch -- so there is no "base" term here and no double edge.
+    // If the overlay is absent, the adjustments are deltas from the game's
+    // default layout, which is still correct behaviour for an editor.
+    // Priority: hidden first (true hide via flags bit 0, applied below),
+    // then manual. No manual setting means identity (no-op).
+    int manual = (manual_mask >> idx) & 1u;
+    int hidden = (hidden_mask >> idx) & 1u;
+    float tdx = 0.0f, tdy = 0.0f, tk = 1.0f;
+    if (manual && params) {
+        tdx = hud_edge_clamp_off(params[idx * 3 + 0], 640.0f);
+        tdy = hud_edge_clamp_off(params[idx * 3 + 1], 360.0f);
+        tk = hud_edge_clamp_k(params[idx * 3 + 2]);
+    }
+    // (0137) `base` and HUD_COMPASS_EXTRA are unused here: the edge anchor
+    // lives in the 0136 overlay. They are kept in the file (the defines)
+    // only so this file still shares the 0135 table/matching code verbatim.
+    // (0137) Every behaviour is a DISPLAYED-position move, applied as a
+    // GLOBAL-matrix delta: the +0x70 m[3]/m[7] are shifted by the effective
+    // delta and m[0]/m[1]/m[4]/m[5] are scaled by the effective k, always
+    // re-derived from the stored ORIGINAL global (e_mod). The local
+    // (+0x30/+0x34, +0x48/+0x4c) is still written exactly as 0135 did, as
+    // a fallback for any path that recomposes from the local. Hide is a
+    // TRUE hide (flags bit 0 cleared), not a +10000 offset.
+    // The parent compensation: this pane's displayed shift is its parent's
+    // applied shift plus its own global shift, so its effective global delta
+    // is its own target minus the parent's applied delta (read from the
+    // parent's table entry). A parent with no entry, or a fresh entry
+    // (sentinel 1e30f), applied the identity. The parent's Calculate runs
+    // before the child's, so its entry is already written when the child is
+    // processed; a stale entry self-corrects on the next frame. The scale
+    // is the ratio (the child's displayed scale is the parent's applied
+    // scale times the child's global scale).
+    float pm_dx = 0.0f, pm_dy = 0.0f, pm_k = 1.0f;
+    void *parent = *(void **)((const char *)pane + 0x18);
+    if (parent && !((uintptr_t)parent & 7u)) {
+        char *pe = hud_edge_find_entry(table, parent);
+        if (pe) {
+            float pdx = *(const float *)(pe + 104);
+            float pk = *(const float *)(pe + 112);
+            if (pdx != 1e30f && pk != 0.0f && pk == pk) {
+                pm_dx = pdx;
+                pm_dy = *(const float *)(pe + 108);
+                pm_k = pk;
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    float edx = tdx - pm_dx;
+    float edy = tdy - pm_dy;
+    float ek = (pm_k == 1.0f) ? tk : (tk / pm_k);
+
+    // The per-element entry: the pane, its original LOCAL translation and
+    // scale (as the game set them -- the layout's own local, which does not
+    // depend on what any parent did), and the effective local delta actually
+    // applied (edx/edy/ek) plus the hidden flag. Sixteen slots per element
+    // (idx + s * HUD_EDGE_N): up to sixteen layout instances can match the
+    // same element, and one slot per element cannot hold them all (0120,
+    // 0126). The stored transform is compared against the target below, so
+    // a settings change or an aspect change re-applies instead of being
+    // ignored, and a manual element is not re-derived from the automatic
+    // offset. A no-op element (effective delta identity, not hidden) never
+    // takes a slot: the identity early return below fires before any
+    // allocation, so an unmoved element costs nothing.
+    // (0137) e_orig holds the original LOCAL; e_mod (+56, unused under
+    // 0135) holds the original GLOBAL matrix (the 12 floats of +0x70 as
+    // first seen). The probe reads e_orig[3]/[7] and e_dx/e_dy/e_k/e_hidden;
+    // e_mod is the 0137 global reference. The layout record shows the
+    // original local and the applied delta; the live_global and final_m3
+    // are the game's +0x70 at probe time.
+    char *ent = 0;
+    int found = 0;
+    for (int s = 0; s < HUD_EDGE_SLOTS_PER_ELEM; s++) {
+        char *e = (char *)table + (idx + s * HUD_EDGE_N) * HUD_EDGE_ENTRY;
+        if (*(uintptr_t *)(e + 0) == (uintptr_t)pane) { ent = e; found = 1; break; }
+        if (!ent && *(u32 *)(e + 124) == 0) ent = e;   // first free slot
+    }
+    if (!found && !hidden && edx == 0.0f && edy == 0.0f && ek == 1.0f) return;   // no-op: never take a slot
+    if (!ent) ent = (char *)table + idx * HUD_EDGE_ENTRY;   // all taken: reclaim the first (a reload)
+    uintptr_t *e_pane = (uintptr_t *)(ent + 0);
+    float *e_orig = (float *)(ent + 8);
+    float *e_mod = (float *)(ent + 56);
+    float *e_dx = (float *)(ent + 104);
+    float *e_dy = (float *)(ent + 108);
+    float *e_k = (float *)(ent + 112);
+    u32 *e_hidden = (u32 *)(ent + 116);
+    if (*e_pane != (uintptr_t)pane) {   // first sighting, or the layout reloaded
+        *e_pane = (uintptr_t)pane;
+        *(u32 *)(ent + 120) = (u32)idx;
+        *(u32 *)(ent + 124) = 1u;
+        *e_dx = 1e30f;   // sentinel: force a recompute below (the stored transform is stale).
+        // e_dy / e_k / e_hidden need no sentinel: the early return below
+        // tests e_dx first (&& short-circuits), so a stale e_dx alone forces
+        // the recompute that writes all four. (Writing them here as adjacent
+        // stores would let the compiler merge them into one 64-bit store of
+        // a constant it loads from .rodata -- a relocation the embedded
+        // words must not carry. The single 1e30f store is the 0118 pattern,
+        // which compiles to movz/movk, relocation-free.)
+        for (int i = 0; i < 12; i++) e_orig[i] = 0.0f;
+        for (int i = 0; i < 12; i++) e_mod[i] = 0.0f;
+        // Store the original local translation and scale (the game's own
+        // values, before any shift). (0135) e_orig is the original LOCAL,
+        // not a global matrix.
+        e_orig[3] = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x30);
+        e_orig[7] = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x34);
+        e_orig[0] = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x48);
+        e_orig[5] = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x4c);
+        // (0137) Store the original GLOBAL matrix (+0x70, 12 floats) as
+        // first seen. For a static pane this may be stale, but it is the
+        // best reference available; the recompute check below refreshes it
+        // if the game ever composes a new one.
+        for (int i = 0; i < 12; i++) e_mod[i] = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x70 + i * 4);
+    }
+
+    // Did the game change the local since the last frame? Compare the
+    // current local against the stored original plus the stored applied
+    // delta, and the current local scale against the stored original scale
+    // times the stored k: if they differ, the game wrote a new local (an
+    // animation, a count change, a layout reload), so the stored original
+    // follows it. The current local is the game's own value (an animation
+    // sets it absolutely from the layout's base, so it does not include our
+    // delta), so it becomes the new original and the delta is re-applied
+    // below. If they are the same, the game left our shifted local alone,
+    // and only a transform change needs a rewrite.
+    // (0137) The same check runs on the GLOBAL matrix (+0x70): if the
+    // game's current global differs from the stored original global plus
+    // the stored applied delta/scale, the game recomposed it, so the stored
+    // original global follows the game's new value.
+    int recomputed = 0;
+    float cur_lx = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x30);
+    float cur_ly = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x34);
+    float cur_sx = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x48);
+    float cur_sy = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x4c);
+    float cur_gx = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x70 + 3 * 4);
+    float cur_gy = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x70 + 7 * 4);
+    if (*e_dx == 1e30f) {
+        recomputed = 1;   // fresh entry: must write
+    } else if (cur_lx != e_orig[3] + *e_dx || cur_ly != e_orig[7] + *e_dy ||
+               cur_sx != e_orig[0] * *e_k || cur_sy != e_orig[5] * *e_k) {
+        recomputed = 1;
+        e_orig[3] = cur_lx;
+        e_orig[7] = cur_ly;
+        e_orig[0] = cur_sx;
+        e_orig[5] = cur_sy;
+    }
+    if (!recomputed && (cur_gx != e_mod[3] + *e_dx || cur_gy != e_mod[7] + *e_dy)) {
+        // The game recomposed the global (a layout reload, an animation):
+        // the stored original global follows the game's new value minus
+        // our applied delta, so the next write re-derives correctly.
+        recomputed = 1;
+        e_mod[3] = cur_gx - *e_dx;
+        e_mod[7] = cur_gy - *e_dy;
+        for (int i = 0; i < 12; i++) {
+            if (i == 3 || i == 7) continue;
+            e_mod[i] = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x70 + i * 4);
+        }
+        // Re-derive the scale reference too: the stored k stays, the
+        // matrix basis is refreshed above.
+    }
+    if (!recomputed && *e_dx == edx && *e_dy == edy && *e_k == ek && *e_hidden == (u32)hidden) return;   // already correct: nothing to do
+
+    // The local shift is always re-derived from the stored original, so a
+    // fixed offset cannot drift: local = original local + effective delta,
+    // local scale = original local scale * effective k. Hidden: the local
+    // x pushed +10000 (the displayed position moves off-screen; the size
+    // does not matter off-screen). A child that wants the same transform as
+    // its parent takes a zero effective delta here and rides the parent
+    // (its local is restored to the original, so nothing is written twice).
+    *(float *)((char *)pane + 0x30) = e_orig[3] + edx;
+    *(float *)((char *)pane + 0x34) = e_orig[7] + edy;
+    *(float *)((char *)pane + 0x48) = e_orig[0] * ek;
+    *(float *)((char *)pane + 0x4c) = e_orig[5] * ek;
+    // (0137) The GLOBAL matrix (+0x70) is written directly, re-derived
+    // from the stored original global: m[3]/m[7] shift by the effective
+    // delta, and the linear part scales by the effective k. This is the
+    // write that reaches the display -- it runs at the +0x13c cut, after
+    // the game's global is final, so it is what Draw renders and what the
+    // children compose from. (A static pane's +0x70 may be stale; the
+    // local write above is the fallback for that path.)
+    {
+        float *gm = (float *)((char *)pane + 0x70);
+        gm[0] = e_mod[0] * ek;
+        gm[1] = e_mod[1] * ek;
+        gm[3] = e_mod[3] + edx;
+        gm[4] = e_mod[4] * ek;
+        gm[5] = e_mod[5] * ek;
+        gm[7] = e_mod[7] + edy;
+    }
+    // (0137) TRUE hide: clear the visible bit (flags bit 0 at +0x58) to
+    // hide, set it to show. This is a real visibility toggle, not an
+    // off-screen move -- the .bflyt finding is that flags=0x00 panes are
+    // never drawn.
+    {
+        unsigned short *pflags = (unsigned short *)((char *)pane + 0x58);
+        if (hidden) *pflags = (unsigned short)(*pflags & (unsigned short)~0x0001u);
+        else *pflags = (unsigned short)(*pflags | 0x0001u);
+    }
+    *e_dx = edx;
+    *e_dy = edy;
+    *e_k = ek;
+    *e_hidden = (u32)hidden;
+
+    // Mark this pane dirty (flags bit 4 at +0x58), so the game recomposes
+    // its +0x70 from the shifted local in the same Calculate call (the
+    // entry cut runs before the flags load, so the flag is visible to the
+    // skip-path check). Its children are calculated after this hook, so
+    // they inherit the moved parent -- but only if the game recomputes
+    // them: mark the descendants dirty too and the game recomputes them
+    // from the moved parent on the same frame.
+    *(unsigned short *)((char *)pane + 0x58) |= 0x10u;
+    hud_edge_mark_dirty(pane);
+}
